<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodoric II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 466</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodoric II was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> from 453 to 466, having taken power by killing his brother Thorismund. An ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he backed the elevation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a> as emperor in 455 and in 456 destroyed the Suebic army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a>, later executing its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechiar">Rechiar</a>. He was murdered by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a>, who succeeded him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Theodoric succeeded his brother Thorismund in 453 after a brief reign of which little is known; it is only known that Theodoric had killed him. He began as a faithful ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Flavius Aetius</a> in 454, the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a> on 16 March 455 and the Vandal sack of Rome on 22 May, the Roman senator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a>, who was at Theodoric's court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and had been his mentor in youth, was urged by the king to claim the imperial crown. Theodoric supported him with a large contingent of warriors.</p><p>In 456 a Gothic army went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on the emperor's behalf. Theodoric attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechiar">Rechiar</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>, to punish his raids on Roman territory, and defeated him at the Battle of Órbigo near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a>. Rechiar was wounded, escaped, and was hunted down and killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Bracara Augusta</a> (Braga), the Suebi's capital, and by tradition plundered several cities of Gallaecia. Afterwards they controlled Hispania Baetica, most of Tarraconensis and southern Lusitania.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a> was unpopular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where he was seen as a puppet of Theodoric. Ricimer and Majorian defeated him near Piacenza in 456, and he was deposed. Theodoric then began to act against Roman authority, leaving soldier colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Majorian marched against him and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>; Theodoric narrowly escaped, and the Goths gave up their settlements in Spain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Majorian's fall in 461 Theodoric gave troops to Ricimer in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> and its surroundings, and took the land south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegidius">Aegidius</a> withdrew northwards. In 463 he sent his younger brother Frederic against Aegidius; Frederic was killed at the Battle of Orleans. Theodoric was himself murdered in 466 by his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a>, who succeeded him.</p><p>The Gallo-Roman Sidonius Apollinaris described Theodoric and his court in a letter to his brother-in-law Agricola. He portrays a king of above average height who heard envoys in the morning, hunted, kept a plain table and enjoyed board games, and he doubts that the king's piety was more than habit. The letter was later largely copied by the chronicler Rahewin for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theodoric II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 456 the Visigothic king <b>Theodoric II</b>, acting in the name of the emperor Avitus, crushed the Suebi, and Visigothic power in Hispania grew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Theodoric II</b> (453–466), sent into Hispania by the emperor Avitus, defeated the Suebic king Rechiarius on the river Órbigo in 456, sacked Bracara Augusta and took Baetica, Carthaginiensis and southern Lusitania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.6,1.44&amp;year=456" aria-label="Toulouse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4585 618 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Theodoric II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5035 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5035 888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.6,1.44&amp;year=456">What was here in 456 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
